WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.780
A laptop at home has the address 192.168.1.23.

2
00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:11.215
It opens a connection to a server on the internet.

3
00:00:11.215 --> 00:00:13.344
Where does the reply go?

4
00:00:13.533 --> 00:00:14.167
Nowhere.

5
00:00:14.167 --> 00:00:17.810
Addresses that start with 192.168 are private.

6
00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:24.305
Millions of homes reuse them, so no router on the internet carries packets to one.

7
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:27.069
So the home router owns one public address.

8
00:00:27.069 --> 00:00:31.191
As the packet leaves, the router rewrites its source to that address.

9
00:00:31.367 --> 00:00:38.072
Then it writes down what it did: the inside address and port, the outside address and port, and the remote end.

10
00:00:38.233 --> 00:00:40.866
The server replies to the router's address.

11
00:00:40.866 --> 00:00:45.457
The router finds the entry and rewrites the destination back to the laptop.

12
00:00:45.667 --> 00:00:50.051
Now the phone also connects from port 41000 to the same server.

13
00:00:50.051 --> 00:00:55.131
Rewrite only the address, and its packets look exactly like the laptop's.

14
00:00:55.333 --> 00:00:57.945
So the router rewrites the port too.

15
00:00:57.945 --> 00:01:02.877
The phone's connection leaves from port 3417 and gets its own entry.

16
00:01:03.067 --> 00:01:09.424
Each reply is matched by its port: 41000 goes to the laptop, 3417 to the phone.

17
00:01:09.424 --> 00:01:13.448
That's how thousands of devices share one address.

18
00:01:13.633 --> 00:01:17.708
Now a packet arrives from outside that nobody inside asked for.

19
00:01:17.708 --> 00:01:22.948
No entry matches, so the router doesn't know which device it's for, and drops it.

20
00:01:23.133 --> 00:01:29.817
Letting traffic in takes a port forward set up by hand, or a load balancer with its own public address.

21
00:01:30.033 --> 00:01:31.389
Entries also expire.

22
00:01:31.389 --> 00:01:37.492
When a connection sits idle too long, the router deletes its entry, and tells neither end.

23
00:01:37.667 --> 00:01:41.999
The laptop's next packet on it matches nothing, and is dropped or reset.

24
00:01:41.999 --> 00:01:45.851
That's the reset on a pooled connection that worked minutes ago.

25
00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:49.635
NAT works because the router remembers every conversation.

26
00:01:49.635 --> 00:01:52.553
A packet it has no memory of has nowhere to go.
